.TH SET_FIND 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
set_find \- Find an element in a set

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/set.h>

.IB bucket_t**
.IB set_find(const set_t* set, const void* val, const size_t size);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBset_find\fR function finds an element in a set. It takes three arguments: the set, a pointer to the element, and the size of the element. The function returns a pointer to the bucket containing the element, or NULL if the element is not found.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB set
The set in which to find the element.
.TP
.IB val
A pointer to the element to find.
.TP
.IB size
The size of the element.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns a pointer to the bucket containing the element, or NULL if the element is not found.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBset_find\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/set.h>

int main() {
    set_t set;
    char* element = "apple";

    set_init(&set);
    set_add(&set, element, strlen(element));

    bucket_t** bucket = set_find(&set, element, strlen(element));

    if (bucket) {
        printf("Element found in bucket %p\n", *bucket);
    } else {
        printf("Element not found\n");
    }

    set_free(&set);
    return 0;
}
.nef